2.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The positive set dad dab gut low cat and the negative set ear cop ego mom ate are all three letter items. The cleanest partition that covers all ten examples is a final-letter partition:

Pos finals = {d,b,t,w} for dad dab gut low cat
Neg finals = {r,p,o,m,e} for ear cop ego mom ate

The set is therefore CVC with third letter ∈ {b,d,t,w}. That is equivalent, on the given material, to CVC free of e,r,p,m anywhere, and in practice to a C a/u/o C shape with a final b/d/t/w.

On the test items
* leg = l e g : CVC, vowel e ∉ {a,u,o}, final g ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* dam = d a m : CVC, vowel a ok, final m ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* rat = r a t : CVC, vowel a ok, final t ∈ {b,d,t,w} → the only item that belongs with the exemplars
* car = c a r : CVC, vowel a ok, final r ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* bin = b i n : CVC, vowel i ∉ {a,u,o}, final n ∉ {b,d,t,w}

Hence rat is the unique conforming option and leg, dam, car, bin are all non-conforming. The disagreement in the room is about which non-conforming exemplar to single out. The e-free / e,r,p,m-free camp singles out leg as the canonical e-containing counter-example and treats bin as the only conforming option under a strict “no e,r,p,m anywhere” reading. The coda-m/p camp singles out dam as the unique m-coda analogue of mom/cop. The final-letter camp treats rat as the odd one in and leg/dam/car/bin as equivalent non-members, with leg often quoted as the first canonical non-member. Unresolved is whether the intended rule is the minimal final-letter rule third∈{b,d,t,w} or the stronger letter-ban no e,r,p,m anywhere; both partition the given ten words perfectly and they diverge only on the test set, notably on bin and rat.

